# A short story for the Aside engine.
#
# EIGHTH REGISTER, AND THE FIRST WITH A DIFFERENT SHAPE. The seven before it are chains: a scene leads to two or
# three, and the ending is whichever branch you took. This one is a HUB. You stand in one room with a shelf, you
# choose what to look at, and you come back. Nothing branches until the end, and the ending does not depend on
# which item you chose - it depends on HOW MANY you bothered to, which is a different question and the reason the
# shape is worth having.
#
# The register is wry, warm and procedural: a lost-property office on disposal day. Unclaimed property is held
# ninety days and then it goes, and the person who runs the office has been logging other people's things for
# eleven years. Each item on the shelf is a small story about somebody who did not come back for it. The last
# item is the first one ever logged, and it has been on the shelf the whole time.
#
# Three endings, and none of them resolves the suitcase.
#
# WHY EVERY ITEM HAS A FLAG AND NOT JUST A COUNTER. The first version of this tracked progress with a single
# `seen` counter and gated the last choice on `seen >= 3`, and the auditor reported it as broken: 7 unreachable
# scenes, 0 paths explored, 0 endings. The reason is in the auditor's own design - it dedupes states by a
# signature of (scene, beat, variables), and it BUCKETS counters, collapsing every value between two thresholds
# into one state. `seen=0` and `seen=1` are the same state, so the search never re-expanded the hub after the
# first item, `seen` never reached 3, and the whole second half of the story was invisible to it.
#
# That is the search being right and the script being wrong. From `office` with seen=0 and seen=1 the OFFERED
# CHOICES were identical, so those two states genuinely had the same future - the story let you pick up the same
# shoe five times. Gating each item on its own flag makes the states differ, which is both what a shelf does and
# what lets the story be walked. A hub needs flags, not a counter.

title: Ninety Days
author: Roxanne
start: office

== office ==
bg lost_office
music fan_hum
The office is a desk, a shelf, and a book. The shelf has five things on it and the book has five lines in it, and
between them they are the whole of the job.

Ninety days. That is the rule and it is a good rule, because it means you never have to decide anything: you
write the date in the book and the date decides for you. Today is disposal day for everything logged in July.

The book is open at July. You have read it already, the way you read it every quarter, and now you do the part
that is not in the rule, which is picking each thing up one last time.

* A single shoe, size six, off the 07:40. -> shoe [if !s_shoe]
* A tin of paint, unopened, with the receipt still taped to it. -> paint [if !s_paint]
* A phone, dead, with four hundred unread messages on it. -> phone [if !s_phone]
* A passport, expired four years. -> passport [if !s_passport]
* A jar of marmalade, homemade, labelled in biro. -> marmalade [if !s_marmalade]
* That is everything. -> shelf [if seen >= 3]

== shoe ==
~ seen +1
~ s_shoe true
A single shoe. Left on the 07:40, which is the 07:40 to the coast, and somebody got off at the coast with one
shoe and did not notice, or noticed and did not care, or was in no state to notice either way.

Size six. Worn through at the outside of the heel, which is how people walk when they are carrying something on
that side. You have thought about that more than the shoe deserves.

* Put it back. -> office

== paint ==
~ seen +1
~ s_paint true
One litre of a colour called Weathered Copper, unopened, with the receipt still taped to the tin and the date on
the receipt four days before it was handed in.

Somebody bought paint, carried it onto a bus, and then had a week in which they did not need it. You do not know
what happened in that week and you never will, and the tin is not going to tell you, and you have decided that
this is the most honest object on the shelf.

* Put it back. -> office

== phone ==
~ seen +1
~ s_phone true
It is dead and it will not charge. Four hundred unread messages, which you know because the lock screen still
shows the count, and the count is the only thing on it that still works.

Four hundred. You have watched that number for ninety days and it has not gone up, and you have decided that
this is because nobody is sending them any more rather than because nobody is receiving them, and you are aware
that you have no evidence for this at all.

* Put it back. -> office

== passport ==
~ seen +1
~ s_passport true
Expired four years ago, which means it was already expired when it was handed in, which means somebody carried an
expired passport around with them and then lost it and then did not come back for it.

There is a photograph in it of a person who is twelve years younger than the person who lost it. You look at the
photograph for slightly longer than you look at the others.

* Put it back. -> office

== marmalade ==
~ seen +1
~ s_marmalade true
A jar of marmalade, homemade, labelled in biro with a date and a name and the word SEVILLE. It is the only thing
on the shelf that somebody made.

It has been here eighty-eight days. You have thought about the marmalade more than any of it, because a jar of
something you made is not the sort of thing you leave on a bus by accident, and because it is the one item on the
shelf that was clearly meant for somebody.

* Put it back. -> office

== shelf ==
There is one more thing on the shelf and it is not in the book, because it was logged before the book.

A suitcase. Eleven years ago, the first week you had this job, handed in off a service that does not run any more.
You wrote it on a page of a different book and then the book changed and it did not get carried over, and so it
has never had a date, and so it has never come up for disposal, and so it has been on that shelf for eleven years
while everything around it came and went.

You have never opened it. You have thought about opening it roughly once a quarter for eleven years.

* Open it. -> open
* Put it in the skip with the rest. -> dispose
* Write today's date on it and give it ninety days. -> keep

== open ==
bg lost_office_open
The catches are stiff and the first one takes a thumbnail with it.

Inside: a man's coat, folded, in a plastic bag that has gone brittle. A paperback with a cracked spine and a
bus ticket for a bookmark, on a page about a third of the way in. A photograph of two people on a beach, in
colour, which dates it. A tin of boiled sweets with three sweets left.

That is all. There is nothing in it that explains anything, and no name anywhere, and the coat is not your size
and never was.

You sit with it for a while. Then you fold the coat back the way it was, because it was folded well and you are
not going to be the one who ruined it.

* -> end_open

== dispose ==
bg lost_office_open
You carry the suitcase out to the skip and put it in, and it lands on the paint and the shoe and the rest of July,
and it makes a sound that is much smaller than eleven years should make.

Back at the desk you write the line in the book that you have been not writing for eleven years: date, description,
disposed. The book is complete now. Every object in this office has a date next to it and there is nothing left on
the shelf, and the job is exactly what it is supposed to be, which is a job with no decisions in it.

* -> end_dispose

== keep ==
You write today's date on the tag and hang it back on the handle, which means it goes on the shelf for another
ninety days, which means in ninety days you will stand here again and do this again.

It is not a decision. It is the absence of one, and you know that, and you have made it eleven years running,
and you have decided that a person is allowed one thing they do not decide about.

* -> end_keep

== end_open ==
bg lost_office_open
The skip goes at four. The shoe goes, and the paint, and the phone with its four hundred messages, and the
expired passport, and the marmalade, which is the only one you hesitate over and which you put in anyway because
eighty-eight days is eighty-eight days.

The suitcase does not go, because it is open now and you cannot put an open suitcase in a skip, and by the time
you have worked out what to do with it the van has gone.

So it is on the shelf, open, with a folded coat and a paperback and a photograph in it, and it will be there
tomorrow, and you have decided that this is fine. It is the first thing anybody ever handed in to you and it is
still here, and that is not the same as it being unclaimed.

-> END

== end_dispose ==
The skip goes at four and it is not full, which is unusual, and the driver says so, and you say yes.

Back at the desk the shelf is empty and the book has six lines in it and the office is a desk, a shelf and a book
with nothing on the shelf and nothing left to decide. You will log something new on Thursday, probably, because
people leave things on buses at a rate that has not changed in eleven years.

You go home at five. On the way out you check the shelf, which you have never done before, and there is nothing
on it, which you knew.

-> END

== end_keep ==
The skip goes at four without the suitcase in it, and the driver does not say anything, because he has never
known what is on your shelf and is not going to start now.

It has ninety days. In ninety days it will come up, and you will stand here with the book open, and you will
write another date on it, and that will be the twelfth time.

You have decided that this is not a failure. The rule exists so that somebody else's things do not become your
problem, and the suitcase is not a problem. It is just a thing that is here, on a shelf, in a room you are in
every day, and you have decided that a room you are in every day is allowed to have one thing in it that you
have not finished with.

-> END
